what you are currently watching is a  sped up version of me playing some brain  training games which i do every morning  there are many different phone apps just  like this one which have similar games  and they're characterized into different  areas of brain cognition such as maths  learning attention speed memory and  problem solving  that's one of the reasons why i like use  on this app as i can see my score in  each of these different areas  now my scores probably look quite good  but i've had over a year of practice now  and the results don't really surprise me  my brain is quite fast i like to  problem-solve and it's flexible  and my language and memory let me down  but have i genuinely improved my mental  ability over time or have i just got  really good at playing the games on  these apps  well let's discuss  our brains represent only two percent of  our body weight get it receives 15  percent of our cardiac output  20  total body oxygen consumption and 25  total body glucose utilization  if you take care of your brain your  brain will take care of you  this is a quote i took from dave  asprey's book superhuman which i read  over two years ago now and ultimately it  goes along with what most people know  our brains are important for our daily  function our working memory our  executive function  and our unconscious activities however  unfortunately as we age our risk for  neurodegenerative diseases increase we  lose white matter in our brain and our  memory declines  so you bring in brain training  brain training refers to activities  purported to maintain or improve one's  cognitive ability  but whether it's apps like this chess  games sudokus or daily crosswords  does brain training provide benefits or  protect against neurodegeneration  well i was on the case  and while it didn't start too well it  didn't take me very long to find this  publication that had this to say  based on this examination  we find extensive evidence that brain  training interventions improve  performance on the trained tasks  less evidence that such interventions  improve performance on closely related  tasks and little evidence that training  enhances performance on distantly  related tasks or that training improves  everyday cognitive performance  and then i found another study  effectively reaching the same conclusion  this one was a consensus signed by 70 of  the world's leading cognitive  psychologists and neuroscientists in  2014  and their verdict was this  the strong consensus of this group is  that the scientific literature does not  support claims that the use of  software-based brain games alters neural  functioning in ways that improve general  cognitive performance in everyday life  or prevent cognitive slowing and brain  disease  so there we have it job done no known  benefits  but it's me so i didn't stop there and  actually there was a response to this  consensus letter  this response was published on the  website brainhq whereby they had this to  say there are now more than 70  peer-reviewed papers about the benefits  of the brain hq exercises from posit  science  more than 50 of those papers focus  specifically on the benefits of our  cognitive exercises and aging  now one common criticism with these  games is that brain training makes you  really good at the game you're doing but  it doesn't transfer to real life common  tasks the consensus is that these brain  training apps may lead to near transfer  for related tasks but there is  disagreement about the potential for far  transfer that would make people more  resilient to cognitive decline so i  decided to take a look at one of these  studies that seemed recent and most  relevant to the apps that i've been  using  improvement in executive function for  older adults through smartphone apps a  randomized clinical trial comparing  language learning and brain training  in this randomized clinical trial they  had 76 adults aged 65 to 75 assigned to  either 16 weeks of spanish learning  using the app during lingo or doing the  equivalent amount of brain training  using the app brainhq or there was a  control condition  and so for any of you who follow me on  twitter you probably know that i also  use duolingo watashiwa ringo does  anyway in this study they were using it  to examine executive function and so  this would be an example of near  transfer for the brain training apps but  far transfer for the people doing  duolingo and what they found was for  their two primary measures duolingo  provided equivalent benefits to brain hq  but brain hq showed a quicker reaction  time and these results were in line with  the previous ideas of language learning  that it's a demanding mental activity  involving working memory long-term  memory sustained attention and auditory  processing  and so it could provide a more effective  mental workout than other activities  and also these app-based language  training  are well at least i find them quite  enjoyable but they also have the  potential to lead to further life  enhancing activities such as taking  advantage of these new languages such as  for travel or for friendship but anyway  i didn't have time to read all 70 of  these papers and as i've been learning  about in the book i'm currently reading  how spies think i probably have some  confirmation bias in my search for  evidence  so to conclude this video and for a  search of alternative strategies to  brain training i turned to the book  better with age the psychology of  successful aging  in this book dr alan castell professor  in the department of psychology at ucla  dedicates an entire chapter to  addressing the question of can computer  games make us smarter  his verdict firstly agrees generally  with that of the consensus  computer-based training holds tremendous  promise as a way to challenge our minds  but currently there is not enough  evidence that computer-based brain  training can help us improve our memory  help us to remember names better or find  our keys  but what i like about this chapter is dr  castell also raises some important pros  and cons  the pros being that these apps and brain  training games help to stimulate and  reward our brain  and apparently they can be beneficial if  you believe it works and such placebo  effects can be powerful  however there are some things you should  be aware of  firstly you shouldn't use up your time  to do these games  if you're sacrificing the time spent on  exercise which have more evidence to  support brain cognition  in addition these games can lead to  additional screen time being sedentary  and socializing less with others the  offsetting costs here may be greater  than the actual benefits of doing these  brain training games  and in addition to exercise reading and  walking for emotional skills and  physical benefits respectively may be  better alternatives  so will i continue given the lack of  evidence  uh well yes mainly because i enjoy  playing it and i try to make sure it's  time i would have otherwise been spent  probably on social media so in my case i  think it's probably beneficial  and to some extent this is still an  emerging field and it would be hard at  least i would think so to even conduct a  study that could fairly assess the  impacts of activities like this on brain  cognition  moreover because these studies are  really hard to control for and are  either too short or too small  and so if you're like me and enjoy doing  these brain training games then why not  it doesn't cost me anything to do it  just don't assume that it's going to be  beneficial  so anyway i hope you've enjoyed this  video thank you to my patreon supporters  and thank you for listening
 

